A tail is published only once it has landed

Two places advanced journal->published_start immediately after calling
jbd_write_sb and without looking at what it returned. If that write failed
the tail was recorded as published anyway -- and the first of the two
branches fires only when published_start has fallen behind start, so
believing it abreast suppresses the very write that would have corrected
it. Recovery then reads from a position the superblock on the medium does
not name.

Both comments already explain why the ordering matters, in detail and
from measurement: a torn image whose superblock said "start at 361,
sequence 607" while block 361 held sequence 936 replays nothing at all.
The write's own result was the one part not consulted.

published_start now moves only when the write returned EOK. A failure
leaves it where it was, which costs one more attempt next lap round the
ring -- the cheap outcome, against a journal that cannot be replayed.

Found by auditing the carried-over backlog rather than from a failure:
the item said "a journal superblock write whose failure still advances
the published tail", and it was accurate.

--- a/src/ext4_journal.c
+++ b/src/ext4_journal.c
@@ -2470,9 +2470,16 @@
 		 * not imagined -- the trace of the failing cut shows exactly
 		 * the journal superblock dropped while reused log space
 		 * landed. */
-		jbd_write_sb(journal->jbd_fs);
-		(void)ext4_block_barrier(journal->jbd_fs->bdev);
-		journal->published_start = journal->start;
+		/* Only a write that landed publishes anything. Advancing
+		 * published_start after a failed one records a tail the medium
+		 * never received; the branch above then sees published_start
+		 * abreast of start and skips the write that would have fixed
+		 * it, so recovery reads from a position the superblock does not
+		 * name. Leaving it put costs one more attempt next lap. */
+		if (jbd_write_sb(journal->jbd_fs) == EOK) {
+			(void)ext4_block_barrier(journal->jbd_fs->bdev);
+			journal->published_start = journal->start;
+		}
 	}
 
 	/* The other way the head reaches the tail: `start` kept pace in
@@ -2486,9 +2493,16 @@
 	if (journal->last == journal->published_start &&
 	    journal->last != journal->start) {
 		(void)ext4_block_barrier(journal->jbd_fs->bdev);
-		jbd_write_sb(journal->jbd_fs);
-		(void)ext4_block_barrier(journal->jbd_fs->bdev);
-		journal->published_start = journal->start;
+		/* Only a write that landed publishes anything. Advancing
+		 * published_start after a failed one records a tail the medium
+		 * never received; the branch above then sees published_start
+		 * abreast of start and skips the write that would have fixed
+		 * it, so recovery reads from a position the superblock does not
+		 * name. Leaving it put costs one more attempt next lap. */
+		if (jbd_write_sb(journal->jbd_fs) == EOK) {
+			(void)ext4_block_barrier(journal->jbd_fs->bdev);
+			journal->published_start = journal->start;
+		}
 	}
 
 	return start_block;
